The double zero and simple zero give $P(x)=k(x-2)^2(x+1)$.
$\begin{align*}-4&=k(-2)^2(1)\\k&=-1\\P(x)&=-(x-2)^2(x+1)\\&=-x^3+3x^2-4\end{align*}$
For stationary points,
$\begin{align*}P'(x)&=-3x^2+6x\\&=-3x(x-2)\\P'(x)&=0\\x&=0\text{ or }2.\end{align*}$
$P''(x)=-6x+6$. Since $P''(0)=6>0$, $(0,-4)$ is a minimum. Since $P''(2)=-6<0$, $(2,0)$ is a maximum.
For the point of inflection,
$\begin{align*}P''(x)&=0\\-6x+6&=0\\x&=1\\P(1)&=-2.\end{align*}$
$P''$ changes from positive to negative at $x=1$, so $(1,-2)$ is a point of inflection.
The curve cuts the axis at $(-1,0)$ and touches it at $(2,0)$. The leading term is $-x^3$, so $P(x)\to\infty$ as $x\to-\infty$ and $P(x)\to-\infty$ as $x\to\infty$.
