\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black,anchor=north west] at (axis cs:1,-2) {$(1,-2)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Both $x=-2$ and $x=2$ have multiplicity $2$, so the curve touches the axis at both. The $y$-intercept is $(0,16)$.
$\begin{align*}P(x)&=(x^2-4)^2\\&=x^4-8x^2+16\\P'(x)&=4x^3-16x\\&=4x(x-2)(x+2)\\P'(x)&=0\\x&=-2,\ 0\text{ or }2.\end{align*}$
$P''(x)=12x^2-16$. Since $P''(-2)=P''(2)=32>0$, $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ are minima. Since $P''(0)=-16<0$, $(0,16)$ is a maximum.
The leading term is $x^4$, so $P(x)\to\infty$ as $x\to\pm\infty$.
