\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black,anchor=north] at (axis cs:2,0) {$(2,0)$};
\node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black,anchor=south] at (axis cs:0,16) {$(0,16)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$x=-1$ is a triple zero, so the curve crosses at a horizontal point of inflection. $x=3$ is a simple zero, so the curve cuts the axis there. The $y$-intercept is $(0,3)$.
Using the product rule,
$\begin{align*}P'(x)&=-3(x+1)^2(x-3)-(x+1)^3\\&=-(x+1)^2(3x-9+x+1)\\&=-4(x+1)^2(x-2)\\P'(x)&=0\\x&=-1\text{ or }2.\end{align*}$
$P'$ is positive on both sides of $x=-1$, so $(-1,0)$ is a stationary point where the curve keeps increasing. Its triple multiplicity establishes the horizontal inflection.
At $x=2$, $P'$ changes from positive to negative, so there is a maximum.
$\begin{align*}P(2)&=-(3)^3(-1)\\&=27.\end{align*}$
The maximum is $(2,27)$. The leading term is $-x^4$, so $P(x)\to-\infty$ as $x\to\pm\infty$.
