$x=-1$ has multiplicity $3$, so the curve crosses the axis at the horizontal inflection $(-1,0)$. $x=1$ has multiplicity $2$, so the curve touches the axis there. The $y$-intercept is $(0,1)$.
The squared factor is positive away from $x=1$, so the sign of $P(x)$ follows the sign of $(x+1)^3$. The curve is below the axis for $x<-1$ and above it for $x>-1$, except at $x=1$.
Calculus confirms the shape:
$\begin{align*}P'(x)&=3(x+1)^2(x-1)^2+2(x+1)^3(x-1)\\&=(x+1)^2(x-1)(5x-1).\end{align*}$
$P'$ is positive on both sides of $x=-1$. It changes from positive to negative at $x=\frac15$, then from negative to positive at $x=1$. Thus there is a maximum between the intercepts and a minimum at $(1,0)$.
The leading term is $x^5$, so $P(x)\to-\infty$ as $x\to-\infty$ and $P(x)\to\infty$ as $x\to\infty$.
